# The sshd configuration under test: what "oidc-ssh enroll" suggests, plus
# the port and address a container needs.
#
# AuthenticationMethods takes alternatives separated by spaces, and every
# method inside one alternative must succeed. "publickey
# keyboard-interactive:pam" therefore means "a key the provider vouches for
# is enough, and a login without one falls back to the device flow", which
# is what the scenarios exercise. Joining the two with a comma would demand
# both, which is the two-factor variant of the same block.
Port 2222
ListenAddress 127.0.0.1
UsePAM yes
KbdInteractiveAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
LoginGraceTime 60
LogLevel DEBUG1

AuthorizedKeysCommand /usr/libexec/oidc-ssh/oidc-ssh authorized-keys %u %f
AuthorizedKeysCommandUser oidc-ssh
# Only these two may come from an authorized_keys "environment=" option. The
# provider is a remote party: anything else it sends, sshd must drop.
PermitUserEnvironment OIDC_USER,OIDC_SUB

Match User systems,ops
    AuthenticationMethods publickey keyboard-interactive:pam
    PubkeyAuthentication yes
    AuthorizedKeysFile none
